# media-render runtime (PRD-251 D3): the direct dependencies, pinned exactly so
# every build of the image renders the same way.
#
# The image installs requirements.lock, never this file (PRD-262 US-053): every
# pin there, the transitive ones included, carries its hashes, and the Dockerfile's
# `py` stage installs it with uv pip install --require-hashes --no-deps.
# --universal resolves for every platform, because the lock installs on
# linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 whatever machine compiles it. Regenerate it from the
# repository root after changing this file:
#
#   uv pip compile services/media-render/requirements.in \
#     --universal --python-version 3.12 --generate-hashes \
#     --no-emit-package protobuf --no-emit-package flatbuffers \
#     -o services/media-render/requirements.lock
#
# PRD-262 #18: onnxruntime requires protobuf and flatbuffers, but only its
# transformers, quantization and tools subpackages import them, and the image
# deletes those; the service loads onnxruntime.capi alone. So they stay out of the
# lock (about 3.1 MB). Dependabot's uv updater does not pass --no-emit-package, so
# a lock it regenerates carries them again, and
# orchestrator/tests/test_prd262_media_render_venv_trim.py fails until the lock is
# compiled again with the command above.

